<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<handout xml:id="conditional-reminders">
  <title>Conditional statement summary</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      Here are some reminders about conditional statements.
    </p>
  </introduction>


  <p>Here is our original conditional statement: <q>If <m>x = 3</m>, then <m>x^{2} = 9</m>.</q></p>
  
  <p>This statement is true (but in general, we can do everything below here even for false statements).</p>
  
  <ul>
  	<li><title>Converse</title> <p><q>If <m>x^{2} = 9</m> then <m>x = 3</m>.</q></p> <p>False - but it may be true or false, depending on the specific statement. In general, you can never trust the converse!</p></li>
	
	<li><title>Contrapositive</title> <p><q>If <m>x^{2} \neq 9</m> then <m>x \neq 3</m>.</q></p> <p>True - the contrapositive is always equivalent to the original conditional statement.</p></li>
	
	<li><title>Biconditional</title> <p><q><m>x = 3</m> if and only if <m>x^{2} = 9</m>.</q></p> <p>False - this can also be written <m>P \leftrightarrow Q</m>. If the original and converse are both true, this would be true too!</p></li>
	
	<li><title>Negation</title> <p><q><m>x = 3</m> and <m>x^{2} \neq 9</m>.</q></p> <p>False - always has the opposite truth value from the original statement.</p></li>
  </ul>
  
</handout>
